  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA passing coastal low to the east of the area will bring some\nclouds for Sunday morning, clearing to the east in the \nafternoon. Rain chances have decreased with a more eastern track\nand dry air in place, highest at only around 10% in eastern \nTucker County. Brief breaks into sun are expected in the \nafternoon before more clouds return on a cold front Sunday \nnight into early Monday. \n\nThis front is expected to clear most of the area before \nsunrise, with rain chances lingering some southeast of \nPittsburgh after sunrise. Rain showers are expected to be \nscattered and mostly confined to the frontal band. Amounts less \nthan 0.1\" are favored, with chances of >0.25\" less than 10%. \nThis cold front will keep Monday highs more seasonable, and \nabout 15F colder than the day prior. Some lake- enhanced \nrain/snow showers are possible north of I-80 Monday night, but \nsnow accumulations will be light to none, with a 90% chance of \nless than 0.5\", if any.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA western CONUS shortwave trough is expected to dig into the\nMidwest on Wednesday, cutting off from the flow. Coincidentally,\na coastal low is still expected to develop and ride up the \neastern seaboard. There still remains a couple scenarios for \nthis system, but it is becoming increasingly confident that both\nof these scenarios spell minimal to no winter weather impacts. \nChances of >1\" of snow accumulation are less than 15% \neverywhere.\n\nIn one scenario, the 500mb trough digs farther south, causing\nthe progression of the coastal low to slow and try to occlude\nunder the upper low over Pennsylvania. This would bring a longer\nresidence time and likely more QPF to the region, but likely\nstill too warm for much snow accumulation perhaps maybe light\naccumulations in NW flow behind the low departure. Rain totals\nin this scenario may be as high as 2\" in spots, and \nThanksgiving may be a bit of a washout with scattered showers \nFriday.\n\nIn the other scenario, the 500mb low is displaced farther \nnorth, occlusion is not expected, and the trough passage between\nthe upper and surface low progresses faster, and has lower QPF \nand generally warmer temperatures that should preclude any \nsnow. QPF in this scenario may be closer to 0.5\" to 1\" in \nspots, with most rain chances Thursday with lesser rain chances\nFriday.",
